Title: Rescue git recency before the frontmatter sweep destroys it
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-10T16:34Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

**This ticket expires.** It is free today and impossible after `0911` lands.

The score needs a recency signal and the corpus has none that survives. No body
carries a date in frontmatter — zero of roughly 950. `.provenance.json` is
batch-stamped: 972 entries share 168 distinct `first_seen` values, one of them
across 78 entries, so ordering on it is run order followed by slug alphabet.
File mtime is destroyed by every clone and by the daily pull. That leaves git
history as the only stable source.

And `0911` rewrites 954 files. The moment it merges, git last-commit recency is
reset corpus-wide and cannot be recovered from the repository. So the snapshot
has to be taken first, whatever is later decided about how to use it.

Design reference: P4 in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`. Review: F6 in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-review-fable-design.md`.

## Relevant files

- `memory/.provenance.json` — the durable place a date can live
- `projects/*/memory/*.md` and `memory/*.md` — the corpus being dated

## Actions

1. For every live body, extract from git history both the **first-commit** and
   the **last-commit** date. First-commit is creation; last-commit is the
   recency that `0911` is about to destroy.
2. Write both into the durable store, distinct from the batch-stamped
   `first_seen` / `last_confirmed` already there — those record when a dream run
   noticed an entry, which is a different fact and should not be overwritten.
3. Record how many bodies got each date, and how many could not be dated.

## Test

Red first: a body whose git history is known has both dates recorded and they
match `git log` for that path. Then a guard against the trap itself — a test
that fails if a body has no recorded creation date, so the coverage cannot
silently regress.

## Verification

- [ ] Every live body carries both dates, or is listed as undatable with a reason
- [ ] The recorded dates match `git log` for a spot-check sample
- [ ] Existing `first_seen` / `last_confirmed` are unmodified

## Invariants

- This ticket adds fields. It changes no body content and deletes nothing.
- It must merge **before** `0911`. That ordering is the whole point.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] First-commit and last-commit dates recorded for every live body
- [ ] Coverage stated as a count, with the undatable listed
- [ ] A test prevents silent regression of the coverage
- [ ] Merged before `0911`
